<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konrad I of Oleśnica</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1294–1366</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Konrad I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a> was a Silesian Piast duke, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-glogow">Henry III of Głogów</a>. In the division of his father's lands he received Oleśnica, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, and founded the Duchy of Oleśnica, which he ruled until 1366. In 1329 he accepted the overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, like most Silesian dukes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-olesnica.jpg" alt="Konrad I of Oleśnica" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konrad_I_Ole%C5%9Bnicki.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Konrad was the second son of Henry III, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a>, and Matilda, daughter of Albert I, Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>. When his father died in 1309 he succeeded to all his lands with his brothers, but as a minor he stayed under the care of his mother and his elder brother Henry IV the Faithful until 1312. On 3 March 1310 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> he was present when his brothers Henry IV and Bolesław renounced their rights over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Gdańsk Pomerania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> for money.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the division of 29 February 1312 Konrad and Bolesław received as co-rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a>, Namysłów and Kluczbork with the regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, while their mother kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a> as her dower. A year later the two divided their share and Konrad took Namysłów and Kalisz, but he lost Kalisz in 1313 after a revolt of the nobility, and in 1314 Władysław I the Elbow-high took almost all of Greater Poland.</p><p>When Bolesław died without issue in 1321, Konrad inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a>. While Władysław I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-the-generous">Bolesław III the Generous</a> plundered the duchy, he married Elisabeth, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vi-the-good">Henry VI the Good</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, which removed any threat from that duchy. The peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 10 August 1323 gave him Wołów, Lubiąż and Smogorzów but forced him to give Namysłów to Bolesław III.</p><p>In the summer of 1343 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> attacked him, but Konrad defeated the Polish army before the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a> and made peace. He took control of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kozle">Koźle</a> in 1355, shared the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a> with Duke Casimir I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a> from 8 December 1357, and bought Milicz the next year. A dispute with his nephew Henry V of Iron over the inheritance of Jan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scinawa">Ścinawa</a>, begun in 1361, was settled in 1365 through Ludwik I the Fair, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Konrad died on 22 December 1366 and was buried in the Chapel of St. Hedwig in the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trzebnica">Trzebnica</a>. His first marriage was childless. By his second wife, Euphemia, daughter of Władysław, Duke of Koźle-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a>, he had a daughter, Hedwig, married to Duke Nikolaus II of Opawa, and a son, Konrad II the Gray.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Konrad I of Oleśnica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the division of the Duchy of Głogów among the sons of Henry III, <b>Konrad I</b> received Oleśnica (Oels), east of Wrocław, and ruled it as a separate duchy by 1320.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-olesnica">Duchy of Oleśnica</a></li></ul></section>
